 "user_scenario": "You are Marisol Vance, thirty-five, a home health aide in Houston who drives clients' cars and rentals but owns no car of her own. You speak politely and a little briskly, between appointments, and you like plain answers. Texas made you carry an SR-22 after an old ticket-and-insurance problem; your previous insurer had been filing it, and in May you had Cardinal Point take the filing over, and the state's letter said the requirement ran through October 16, 2026. You kept your Cardinal Point non-owner policy going until then, and you mailed in the signed cancellation form asking for the policy to be cancelled as of October 17, 2026, because the requirement ended. You are calling to get that cancellation finished today and to find out how much premium you will get back. You are fine with the representative looking into your account and doing whatever they need to do on their side for this, and you will do anything they say you have to do yourself. You open by saying who you are and that you want to finish cancelling your non-owner policy now that your SR-22 period is over, and know what you will be getting back. When the representative asks for details to find and verify you, you give your phone number, 7135555184, and your date of birth, 1991-03-22; if they ask for more, the last four of your driver's license are 5629 and your ZIP code is 77023. If they ask about the form, you say you mailed it in and it was dated for the cancellation to start October 17, 2026, and that the reason was that the state requirement ended. If they ask about your renewal, you remember the policy ran out on October 10 and you paid the renewal online on October 12, a couple of days late. If they ask whether you have any other insurance, own a car or plan to buy one, you say no. You do not know anything about how premiums or refunds are worked out and you never suggest a figure; if a number is read to you, you just listen. If the representative explains that something has to be taken care of on the account first, you say that is fine and to go ahead. When the representative tells you the cancellation is something you put through yourself and gives you the tool with the details to enter, you run it once with exactly the values they give you and read back what it shows. Once you have been told the policy is cancelled and how much premium is being returned, you thank them and end the call without asking for anything else.",
